    {
      "id": "BPP-03",
      "chapter": "normalization",
      "anchor": "normalization-branching-period-eigenvalue",
      "title": "A projected vector supplies a positive eigenvalue and a smooth unit period vector",
      "hypotheses": "For v=π(g₀)v₀ assume p=i*v≠0, the absolute integrability of the restricted coefficient and of the seed coefficient, square-integrability of σ for ν with degree dσ>0, and the unitary branching inclusion.",
      "statement": "The integrated conjugate coefficient on the smaller Hilbert space is compact self-adjoint, dominates |⟨p,a⟩|²/dσ in quadratic form, and has a smooth normalized eigenvector w with eigenvalue c₁≥‖p‖²/(2dσ)>0. Its period pairing is c₁⟨a,w⟩ for every a.",
      "definitions": [
        "The smoothness uses a bounded intertwiner from the ambient representation to the subgroup model sending v to w after division by c₁. The adjoint P is distinguished from the orthogonal projection iP.",
        "In the normalized circle example the exact eigenvalue is 9/25 and the guaranteed lower bound is 9/50."
      ],
